   {
    "id": "q4",
    "text": "Does a command with a wrong length for its type, such as read with length 0, use up its sequence number?",
    "default": "No, since wrong length is judged before the sequence rule it changes nothing.",
    "reason": "Order of rules places length before sequence.",
    "answer": "No. A command with a wrong length for its type is refused at the length check, which comes before the sequence check, so it never passes the sequence rule and does not consume its sequence number. Under REQ-05, a refused packet changes no sequence state.",
    "status": "answered",
    "by": "oracle",
    "evidence": "Checks in order: long, short, bad CRC (silent drops), type, length, sequence, then register number and access; a command that passes the sequence rule consumes its number."
